  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Laws of Indices"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What laws of indices do I need to know?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- In an expression like $a^{n}$\n\n  - $a$ is known as the **base**\n  - $n$** **is known as the **index** (also called the **power** or **exponent**)\n- The **index laws** you **need to know** and be able to use are summarised here:\n\n  - $a^{m}\\timesa^{n}=a^{m+n}$\n  - $a^{m}\\diva^{n}=a^{m-n}$\n  \n    - Be careful!  The two laws above can only be used if the two terms on the left-hand side of the equation have the **same base**.\n  - $(a^{m})^{n}=a^{mn}$\n  - $(ab)^{n}=a^{n}b^{n}$\n  - $a^{1}=a$\n  - $a^{0}=1$\n  - `a to the power of 1 over m end exponent equals m-th root of a`\n  - `a to the power of m over n end exponent equals n-th root of a to the power of m end root equals open parentheses n-th root of a close parentheses to the power of m`\n  - $a^{-m}=\\frac{1}{a^{m}}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I work with laws of indices?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Laws of indices work with **numerical** and **algebraic** terms\n- These can be used to **simplify expressions** where terms are multiplied or divided\n\n  - Deal with the **number** and **algebraic** parts **separately**\n  \n    - `open parentheses 3 x to the power of 7 close parentheses cross times open parentheses 6 x to the power of 4 close parentheses equals open parentheses 3 cross times 6 close parentheses cross times open parentheses x to the power of 7 cross times x to the power of 4 close parentheses equals 18 x to the power of 11`\n    - $\\frac{3x^{7}}{6x^{4}}=\\frac{3}{6}\\times\\frac{x^{7}}{x^{4}}=\\frac{1}{2}x^{3}$\n    - `open parentheses 3 x to the power of 7 close parentheses squared equals open parentheses 3 close parentheses squared cross times open parentheses x to the power of 7 close parentheses squared equals 9 x to the power of 14`"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How can I solve equations when the unknown is in the index?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- If **two terms with indices are equal** and the terms have **the same positive base **(other than 1) then **the indices must be** **equal**\n\n  - If $a^{x}=a^{y}$ then  $x=y$\n  \n    - Not valid if $a\\leq0$ or $a=1$\n- If the **unknown** is **part of the index** then **write both sides **with** the same base number**\n\n  - Then ignore the base number, **make the indices equal **and** solve** that equation"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "`table row cell 5 to the power of 2 x end exponent end cell equals 125 row cell 5 to the power of 2 x end exponent end cell equals cell 5 cubed end cell row cell 2 x space end cell equals cell space 3 end cell row cell x space end cell equals cell space 3 over 2 end cell end table`"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- In **more complicated questions** you might have to use **negative** and **fractional** indices\n\n  - You may also have to **rewrite** both sides with the **same base number**"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "`table row cell 8 to the power of x end cell equals cell 1 fourth end cell row cell open parentheses 2 cubed close parentheses to the power of x end cell equals cell 1 over 2 squared end cell row cell 2 to the power of 3 x end exponent end cell equals cell 2 to the power of negative 2 end exponent end cell row cell 3 x end cell equals cell negative 2 end cell row x equals cell negative 2 over 3 end cell end table`"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
